WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.013
Lesson 2 becomes much cleaner when the learner names the original flux change first and treats the induced response as a physical act of opposition.

2
00:00:05.013 --> 00:00:10.027
Original change means start here first.

3
00:00:10.027 --> 00:00:15.040
You must decide what is increasing or decreasing before choosing direction.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.053
Induced response means oppose that change.

5
00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:25.067
The loop responds in a direction that resists the change.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:30.080
Energy meaning means not free gain.

7
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.093
Lenz's law protects energy conservation by resisting the change.

8
00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:40.107
Always name the change before the induced response.

9
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:45.120
Keep this takeaway in view: Always name the change before the induced response.

10
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.309
This diagram matters because it prevents the learner from skipping the original change and jumping straight to a current direction.

11
00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:55.498
Increase case means oppose the increase.

12
00:00:55.498 --> 00:01:00.687
If one flux direction is increasing, the induced field points the other way.

13
00:01:00.687 --> 00:01:05.876
Decrease case means oppose the decrease.

14
00:01:05.876 --> 00:01:11.064
If one flux direction is fading, the induced field tries to support it.

15
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:16.253
Loop current means follows from induced field.

16
00:01:16.253 --> 00:01:21.442
Use the right-hand grip rule only after the induced field direction is chosen.

17
00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:26.631
Direction comes from opposition to change, then from grip-rule mapping.

18
00:01:26.631 --> 00:01:31.820
Keep this takeaway in view: Direction comes from opposition to change, then from grip-rule mapping.

19
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.831
A resistance-to-disturbance analogy helps because the system is not trying to erase itself.

20
00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:39.842
It is trying to oppose the new change being imposed on it.

21
00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:43.853
Applied disturbance means original flux change.

22
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:47.864
This is what the loop is reacting to.

23
00:01:47.864 --> 00:01:51.875
Response means opposes the disturbance.

24
00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:55.886
The induced effect acts against the new change.

25
00:01:55.886 --> 00:01:59.897
Lesson gain means energy logic.

26
00:01:59.897 --> 00:02:03.908
Opposition keeps the response physically sensible.

27
00:02:03.908 --> 00:02:07.919
Lenz's law is a response rule grounded in opposition.

28
00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:11.930
Keep this takeaway in view: Lenz's law is a response rule grounded in opposition.

29
00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:17.092
Lesson 2 is strongest when the learner separates how big the induced emf is from which way the induced response points.

30
00:02:17.092 --> 00:02:22.253
First move means identify the original change.

31
00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:27.415
Into page increasing is a different story from into page decreasing.

32
00:02:27.415 --> 00:02:32.577
Second move means choose induced field.

33
00:02:32.577 --> 00:02:37.738
Pick the field that opposes that change.

34
00:02:37.738 --> 00:02:42.900
Third move means map to current.

35
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:48.062
Use the grip rule only after the induced field direction is known.

36
00:02:48.062 --> 00:02:53.223
Keep these relations visible: induced emf = -N delta(Phi) / delta(t); the negative sign encodes opposition to change; and use field direction before current direction.

37
00:02:53.223 --> 00:02:58.385
The sign is a direction cue.

38
00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:03.547
It is not a command to ignore the physical story of what is changing.

39
00:03:03.547 --> 00:03:08.708
Use Faraday for size and Lenz for direction.

40
00:03:08.708 --> 00:03:13.870
Keep this takeaway in view: Use Faraday for size and Lenz for direction.

41
00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:18.293
A dependable Lesson 2 routine begins with the original flux change, then chooses the opposing induced field, then maps that field to current direction.

42
00:03:18.293 --> 00:03:22.717
Step 1 means original change.

43
00:03:22.717 --> 00:03:27.140
Increasing or decreasing, and in which direction?

44
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.563
Step 2 means induced field.

45
00:03:31.563 --> 00:03:35.987
Choose the field that resists that change.

46
00:03:35.987 --> 00:03:40.410
Step 3 means induced current.

47
00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.833
Use the grip rule to convert field direction into loop current.

48
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:49.257
Change first, opposition second, current third.

49
00:03:49.257 --> 00:03:53.680
Keep this takeaway in view: Change first, opposition second, current third.

50
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.440
The increase case becomes easy when the word oppose is taken literally.

51
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.200
Original change means more into-page flux.

52
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.960
That is what the loop is trying to resist.

53
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.720
Induced field means out of the page.

54
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.480
That is the opposing field direction.

55
00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.240
Current result means counterclockwise.

56
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.000
The grip rule then maps field direction to current direction.

57
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.760
Oppose the increase before you name the current.

58
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.520
Keep this takeaway in view: Oppose the increase before you name the current.

59
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.934
A decrease case is not the same as an increase case with words shuffled around.

60
00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:36.348
The response changes because the change changes.

61
00:04:36.348 --> 00:04:40.762
Original change means less into-page flux.

62
00:04:40.762 --> 00:04:45.176
The loop is reacting to a drop.

63
00:04:45.176 --> 00:04:49.590
Induced field means into the page.

64
00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.004
That supports the disappearing original direction.

65
00:04:54.004 --> 00:04:58.418
Current result means clockwise.

66
00:04:58.418 --> 00:05:02.832
Now the grip rule gives the opposite current direction.

67
00:05:02.832 --> 00:05:07.246
Oppose the decrease, not the label of the field alone.

68
00:05:07.246 --> 00:05:11.660
Keep this takeaway in view: Oppose the decrease, not the label of the field alone.

69
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.584
If the induced effect reinforced the original change instead of opposing it, the system would spiral the change up without a sensible energy source.

70
00:05:16.584 --> 00:05:21.509
Opposition means energy guard.

71
00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:26.433
The loop resists the imposed change.

72
00:05:26.433 --> 00:05:31.358
No free growth means physical sanity.

73
00:05:31.358 --> 00:05:36.282
The response is not allowed to amplify the cause for free.

74
00:05:36.282 --> 00:05:41.207
Lesson gain means meaningful direction rule.

75
00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:46.131
Lenz's law is physically grounded, not arbitrary.

76
00:05:46.131 --> 00:05:51.056
Opposition is what keeps the induction response physically sensible.

77
00:05:51.056 --> 00:05:55.980
Keep this takeaway in view: Opposition is what keeps the induction response physically sensible.

78
00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.647
Use this checkpoint to test whether the opposition rule is really active.

79
00:06:00.647 --> 00:06:05.313
Question means into-page flux increasing.

80
00:06:05.313 --> 00:06:09.980
What induced field direction should oppose that increase?

81
00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.647
Route means oppose the change.

82
00:06:14.647 --> 00:06:19.313
The response must resist more into-page flux.

83
00:06:19.313 --> 00:06:23.980
Answer check means out of the page.

84
00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:28.647
That is the induced field direction to choose before mapping the current.

85
00:06:28.647 --> 00:06:33.313
The induced response opposes the increase, not the field label alone.

86
00:06:33.313 --> 00:06:37.980
Keep this takeaway in view: The induced response opposes the increase, not the field label alone.

87
00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:42.349
Carry one route into the worked example.

88
00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:46.718
Name the original flux change, pick the induced field that opposes it, and only then use the grip rule to decide current direction.

89
00:06:46.718 --> 00:06:51.088
Step 1 means change.

90
00:06:51.088 --> 00:06:55.457
Say what is increasing or decreasing.

91
00:06:55.457 --> 00:06:59.826
Step 2 means opposition.

92
00:06:59.826 --> 00:07:04.195
Choose the induced field direction.

93
00:07:04.195 --> 00:07:08.564
Step 3 means current.

94
00:07:08.564 --> 00:07:12.933
Then use the grip rule.

95
00:07:12.933 --> 00:07:17.302
Keep these relations visible: name the original flux change; choose the induced field that opposes it; and map induced field to loop current.

96
00:07:17.302 --> 00:07:21.672
The current direction is the last step, not the first.

97
00:07:21.672 --> 00:07:26.041
Lenz's law becomes easy when the order is respected.

98
00:07:26.041 --> 00:07:30.410
Keep this takeaway in view: Lenz's law becomes easy when the order is respected.
